        rOptDesc_topBottom: "The card just played matches the rank of the card at the very BOTTOM of the pile — the one that started the round. <em>e.g. A → 5 → A</em>. The only pattern that asks you to remember something instead of just watching the top.",
        rOptNote: "You are told before you commit: the House Rules badge in the waiting room and above the pile lists everything a table added or removed.",
        privacyButton: "Privacy",
        aboutButton: "About",
        aTitle: "About This Game",
        aWhatTitle: "🃏 What this is",
        aWhatDesc: "Egyptian Rat Screw is a reflex card game: cards go down one at a time, and when the pile makes a pattern, the first hand to slap it takes the whole stack. This version runs in a browser with no download — one click starts a match against three bots. You only need an account if you want your scores on the leaderboard or want to play against real people.",
        aBuiltTitle: "🛠️ How it is built",
        aBuiltDesc: "Plain JavaScript modules, no framework and no build step: the files your browser downloads are the files that were written. Firebase handles sign-in, the leaderboard and multiplayer rooms. The slap patterns live in one registry that both the browser and the server read, so the two can never disagree about what counts as a slap. A release ships only after the rule engine, the scoring maths and a scripted walkthrough in a real browser all pass.",
        aFairTitle: "⚖️ How it stays fair",
        aFairDesc: "In multiplayer a contested pile does not go to whoever's message reaches the server first. It goes to the lower reaction time, measured from the moment the card landed on the screen, so a faster connection is not an advantage. Joining a table is checked on the server, never in the page. And the Daily Challenge deals every player in the world the same position from the same seed, with your personal settings overridden while it is scored — otherwise an easier setting would buy a higher score on the same board.",
        aDevTitle: "👤 Who made it",
        aDevDesc: "Built and maintained by Berk Elmalı as an independent project. There is no publisher behind it and no team — feature requests, bug reports and disagreements about the rules all arrive in the same inbox.",
        aContactTitle: "✉️ Contact",
        aContactDesc: "Questions, bug reports or a data request: berk9elmali9@gmail.com. If you have found a rules bug, the fastest way to get it fixed is to say which cards were on the pile when it happened.",
        rOriginTitle: "📜 Where the game comes from",
        rOriginDesc: "Egyptian Rat Screw is a slapping variant of <strong>Beggar-My-Neighbour</strong>, an old English children's game in which players lay cards down in turn and a face card forces the next player to pay a penalty. The slapping half comes from the Slapjack family. The combination travels under a long list of names — ERS, ERF, Egyptian War, Bloodystump, Ratslap — and there is no documented account of where the \"Egyptian\" came from. The game spread by being taught at kitchen tables, not by being published, so nobody wrote that part down.",
        rHouseVaryTitle: "🏠 Why everyone's rules are different",
        rHouseVaryDesc: "Because it spread by word of mouth, almost every group plays a slightly different set. Doubles and sandwiches are close to universal; tens, marriages, top-bottom and four-in-a-row are the ones people argue about. Rather than pick a side, this game makes them switches: the four classic patterns are on by default, three more stay off until you turn them on, and the table you are about to sit at shows which set is live <em>before</em> the first card is dealt — so nobody discovers a house rule by losing a pile to it.",
        rStrategyTitle: "🎯 What actually makes you better",
        rStrategyDesc: "A wrong slap costs a card off the bottom of your deck, so the cheapest improvement is not slapping <em>faster</em> — it is slapping less often on patterns you are unsure of. Tens are what people miss: the two cards must add to exactly ten and face cards do not count, which is easy to see one beat too late. Watch the pile grow rather than the card being played; a five-card pile is worth several turns of risk, a two-card pile is not. And while a slap streak is shielding you, a wrong slap breaks the shield instead of burning a card — so on a streak, a marginal slap is worth more than it usually is.",
        rDailyRulesTitle: "🗓️ Why the Daily Challenge is the same for everyone",
        rDailyRulesDesc: "The day's deal is generated from the date itself, not from a roll on the server, so every player in the world starts from the same mid-match position with the same cards in the same hands. The scenario shape and the bot difficulty are functions of the date too. While a scored run is going your own settings are overridden — the turn clock, the animation speed and the rule set are the day's, not yours. Otherwise a gentler setting would buy a higher score on an identical board, and the leaderboard would rank preferences instead of reflexes.",
        pTitle: "Privacy Policy",
        pUpdated: "Last updated: 29 August 2026",
        pAuthTitle: "👤 Account",
        pAuthDesc: "Signing in uses Firebase Authentication. We store the account id it gives us and the display name you choose. If you sign in with email, that email is held by Firebase Authentication. You can play offline against bots without an account at all.",
        pGameTitle: "🎮 Game data",
        pGameDesc: "Your username and scores are stored in Firebase Firestore so leaderboards can exist. Multiplayer matches are coordinated through Firebase Realtime Database and the room data is deleted when the match ends. A Daily Challenge result is written with exactly nine fields: your account id, your username, the score, your reaction time, whether you finished the run, how long it took, the scenario profile you were given, your starting hand size, and the time you submitted it — nothing else.",
        pLocalTitle: "💾 Stored in your browser",
        pLocalDesc: "Settings, house rules, slap statistics, your Daily Challenge result for the day, your daily wheel spin, coins and card skins are kept in your browser's localStorage. They never leave your device unless you are signed in and a score is submitted. Clearing your browser data removes all of it.",
        pAdsTitle: "📢 Advertising",
        pAdsDesc: "This game shows Google AdSense ads on menu and information screens only — never during a match, and never on a Daily Challenge run. Google and its partners may use cookies to serve and measure ads. You can review and change your choices at any time through the consent screen, and you can turn off personalised advertising at google.com/settings/ads.",
        pChoicesTitle: "⚙️ Your choices",
        pChoicesDesc: "You can play without an account, delete your local data by clearing browser storage, reset your slap statistics from the Slap IQ panel, and change your advertising consent at any time. To have an account and its stored scores deleted, contact us below.",
        pContactTitle: "✉️ Contact",
        pContactDesc: "Questions about this policy or a data request: berk9elmali9@gmail.com",
        reflexFaster: "↓ {pct}% faster than your recorded slaps",
